# Line endings are load-bearing for two of these and nothing enforced them.
#
# `deploy.sh` and everything in `ops/` is checked out on the VPS and executed by bash.
# A shell script committed with CRLF fails there as `bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M` — and
# on the deploy path that is not a broken script, it is a site that does not come back.
# Today the repository is safe only because this machine happens to have
# `core.autocrlf=true`, which normalises on commit; a contributor, a container, or a CI
# checkout with `autocrlf=input` or `false` would commit exactly what its editor wrote.
# This makes the guarantee a property of the repository instead of a property of one
# developer's config.
#
# Caught while committing the deploy fixes: files rewritten by a Python script on
# Windows came out CRLF in the working tree, and `git ls-files --eol` was the only thing
# that could say whether that reached the index.
* text=auto

*.sh    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf

*.db    binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.png   binary
